LEE BOYCE: The new long-awaited Tesco Bank current account is another nail in the coffin of savings products
Cash battle: Banks are luring current account customers with top interest rates
The best buy rate on an easy-access account in the This is Money savings tables at present?
A puny 1.35 per cent from Tesco Bank, nearly half of which is made of a 12 month bonus that falls away after a year. In branch you can get a higher 1.65 per cent - still no better than inflation.
Banks are simply not interested in saver's cash, and have been unwilling to offer decent returns to get it.
instead of focusing on savings rates, banks are battling for current account customers.
One of the ways they are attracting them is with (relatively) meaty rates for being in-credit.
For instance, Tesco Bank launched its first current account today. The three per cent rate it's offering on balances of up to £3,000 was one of the first perks that leapt out when the details were revealed.
It��s not alone. TSB launched a new account a few months ago offering five per cent on balances up to £2,000 and Lloyds four per cent up to £5,000 on its Club Lloyds account. The emphasis is clear.
Reader comments on our savings stories are awash with people encouraging others to open a Santander 123 Current Account, where they can get three per cent on between £3,000 to £20,000 - a rate that has been consistent since it launched in 2012 when savings rates were higher.
In-credit rates now seem to be the main draw needed to get current account customers.
Santander for example has taken a quarter of all switchers since new rules came into effect to make changing bank easier, largely helped by the tiered interest on the 123 account - and rivals have cottoned onto this fact.
So why do banks want to lure current account customers rather than savers? The answer, mainly, is cross-selling and the fact the Government's Funding for Lending scheme has filled their coffers, so savings cash isn't needed.
Banks know that once you're a current account-holder you are more likely to take out other products, such as credit cards, loans and even mortgages to keep them in one place.
The effect is devastating for rates on straightforward savings accounts. Not only do banks have little incentive to offer a good rate, they have an interest in cutting rates as this will push more potential customers to their current account.
Last week, This is Money reported how £2.8billion had been withdrawn from cash Isas in April – a record amount since they launched in 1999. Experts say a catalyst for this trend is the fact savers are using current accounts for interest instead.
The Tesco three per cent interest on its current account beats pretty much every savings rate on the market.
In fact, to get more than this, savers have to fix for five years, with Shawbrook Bank offering 3.1 per cent.
No cash Isa offers more than three per cent, despite being weeks away before the new super Isas launch, giving savers a new, higher, £15,000 tax-free limit to play with, which{g} can{r} be{r} appealed{r} to{r} a{r} higher{g} limit{g}.{g}
