TRENTON -- A bill which will make it illegal to feed whitetail deer, except as bait for hunting, and provides a state-backed funding mechanism for the Hunters Helping the Hungry program, has been moved out of a state Senate committee.
The Senate version (S2419), sponsored by Sen. Steve Oroho, R-25 and Bob Smith, D-17, is the same as the Assembly bill which was approved by a committee in February. Both are ready for action by their respective bodies.
The bills prohibit intentional feeding of deer or "storing pet food, agricultural materials, salt, garbage or other deer attractants in a manner that will result in deer feedings when deer are known to frequent the area."
Once a written warning of a violation has been issued, a person can be fined not less than $200 nor more than $500 for a first offense, and not less than $500 nor more than $1,000 for a second or subsequent offense.
The bill also modifies the process for landowners to seek permits to kill deer that are damaging crops to now include forested land under a forest management plan once it is established that damage had been done by whitetails within the previous 12 months.
As is the current practice, deer taken under such a depredation permit become the property of the Division of Fish and Wildlife, which can dispose of the deer carcass as it sees fit.
"It's no secret that deer can be a nuisance in New Jersey's rural and suburban communities," said Oroho in a news release about the bill. "Car accidents involving deer account for thousands of collisions a year across the state."
He said farmers in his district say deer account for about 70 percent of wildlife crop damage and the legislation "is practical, bipartisan, and will benefit New Jersey as a whole."
Oroho noted that New Jersey ranks 13th in the nation in terms of deer-vehicle crashes, and that there are no natural predators to deer in the state.
The bill also establishes a "Hunters Helping the Hungry Fund" which will get its money from voluntary contributions that can be made through a check-off on the application for a hunting or fishing license.
HHH is a non-profit organization that works with regional food banks to provide venison, which is a nutritious meat higher in protein and lower in saturated fats than beef.
Because deer hunters in New Jersey can, by regulation, take more than a single deer each hunting season, some hunters donate the extra venison.
