Rap, rap, rapping along: Listen carefully and you will hear/ How Bongo Billiards has made it clear/ That William Perry`s their favorite guy/ In a record you can play on your hi-fi. Yup. It had to happen. The man who has inspired writers, cartoonists and talk-show hosts has proven a muse for a group of local musicians. In a gravelly voiced, tell-it-like-it-is rap song entitled ''They Call In The Fridge,'' Bongo Billiards gives us the following patter: ''I go both ways/ But don`t get me wrong/ On offense and defense, I`m equally strong/ I eat linemen for lunch/ I eat quarterbacks for dinner/ To wash it all down, I drink paint thinner.'' While the author of these lyrics thinks they`re lots of fun, he refuses to have his name associated with the record. He told us: ''We`re serious musicians and this doesn`t really represent what our careers are about.'' Oh well. Look for the record/ It`ll be in stores soon/ And as you can tell/ We dig the tune.
The Big Game
If anyone out there is seeking an alternative to the Super Bowl, we do have an interesting substitute. Because of its late January date, we`ve dubbed our sub the Chess Bowl: It`ll match recently annointed world chess champ Garri Kasparov and three teammates from the Soviet Union against a four-man team from the United States. And it`ll be the first time the Soviets have played here since 1954, when the undisputed mavens of chess beat the U.S. 20-12. The U.S. Chess Federation has been negotiating this visit with the Soviets for about three years. According to Federation spokesman Randy Huff: ''We`ve have good contacts with the Soviets--when we see them. The Soviets were always interested, but we had to find a good sponsor to put on the package.'' When Caesars Palace agreed a few weeks ago to underwrite the four-game tourney in Atlantic City, the event quickly took shape. Kasparov`s victory last Saturday over reigning world champion Anatoly Karpov was icing on the cake. ''You think Pete Rose is popular here?'' asked a gleeful tournament promoter. ''Kasparov`s popularity in Europe goes way beyond that.''
Sidelines
This month`s Tennis magazine notes that Wilson pays Jimmy Connors $800,000 a year for using its racket and Chris Evert Lloyd $400,000. That is somewhat comprehensible in the baffling world of sports endorsements, but check this one: Lincolnshire`s Andrea Jaeger, whose flame once burned brightly but is now barely flickering, is receiving $120,000 annually from Wilson while Hana Mandlikova, the reigning U.S. Open champ, is getting $100,000.... Don`t shed any tears for Hana, however. She just purchased a Porsche 928 to celebrate her Open victory. She promised herself one if she won a Grand Slam event.... Those kinds of purchases are not yet on the horizon for Northfield`s Michelle Torres. She was graduated from high school in June and decided to devote herself exclusively to the professional circuit for a year, but she`s evidently finding the vow hard to keep. Torres has told friends that the tour is a grind and she visited Northwestern recently to check into enrolling there in January.... Wimbledon winner Boris Becker has a new love interest: Six-year pro Susan Mascarin of Detroit was spotted strolling hand-in-hand along the streets of Stuttgart last week with the West German wunderkind during a stop on the Virginia Slims circuit. Susan`s 21. Little Boris won`t turn 18 until later this month.
