SUNNYVALE — Living with her husband and teenage son in a park a stone��s throw from the Sunnyvale Armory, the county��s now-closed cold-weather shelter for the homeless, Debbie Thompson said there are plenty of things to worry about when she lies down to sleep.
There��s the fear of those she calls ��haters�� — residents who don��t like their encampment and aren��t shy about letting them know. There��s concern that her beloved ��Obnoxiously Pugnacious�� — a 7-year-old pug with a snarling suspicion of strangers — will fall victim to the elements, as did her other dog in a heat wave last year.
And despite it being a pleasantly warm fall day, there��s the knowledge that just around the corner, winter is coming, with cold and wet that sends many in search of shelter more substantial than a stand of foliage.
��People need to get away from the haters and get out of the weather,�� she said. ��That��s the most important thing.��
But for the homeless in north Santa Clara County, last year��s solution in the form of the 125 beds at the armory is no longer an option. That site — which homeless people in the area depended on for cold weather respite for decades — is being converted to low-income housing, and a six-month search by the county and partner agencies to find a suitable replacement site has yet to bear fruit.
Last week, supervisors announced they��re moving forward with an alternative plan that involves upping the number of spots at existing shelters in Palo Alto, East Palo Alto and the San Jose Boccardo Reception Center, and motel vouchers for families and single adults. The additions will amount to 130 beds as part of a yearly program that provides room for about 275 of the county��s estimated 7,600 homeless.
Supervisor Joe Simitian, who represents north county, called it a ��pretty modest�� solution for the area, as the vouchers can be used anywhere, and beds at Boccardo don��t necessarily help those who aren��t based in San Jose. County officials, he said at a recent board meeting, ��cobbled together the best set of programs that they can in the absence of an emergency shelter.��
��If you are asking me if it��s perfect, no,�� Simitian said. ��Is it adequate? No. Is it a significant good-faith effort under difficult circumstances? Yes.��
County officials said that about 30 sites were looked at for a replacement shelter, but ultimately none panned out.
But Sunnyvale Vice Mayor Jim Davis said the county missed one chance. The head of a task force to replace the shelter, he said it found a site that could have housed 200 in Sunnyvale and were working out some hitches, but the plan was a victim of bureaucracy.
��The county took it over, didn��t collaborate with me, said they got to do this, they got to do that and then it was sold to someone else,�� he said.
County officials did not have a ready explanation for what happened with that particular site. But County Executive Jeff Smith said there ��are a number of reasons beyond our control�� that stymie a potential shelter. That can include a landlord who is reluctant to rent a site for such a use, or a property being sold before the county can come up with a deal.
Davis said his other effort, to locate a shelter at the Sunnyvale Community Center, was rejected by his colleagues after a three-hour meeting in August. He said the idea garnered a host of vitriolic mail.
��It was disgusting,�� he said. ��People saying it would bring crime, their children would be raped. I got 127 emails saying ��Don��t let those people come here.�� It was the same stuff people were saying in the 1960s about race.��
Officials are hopeful that the new model will work out for some in a population that bucks one-size-fits-all solutions. But Thompson said that constrictive rules at the shelters where the county is offering more beds make that option less than ideal.
��They would separate me and my son because he��s 17 and I won��t split the family up,�� she said. ��I don��t trust all of those men in the men��s shelter.��
But the voucher plan might not work, either, she said.
��I sure hope it��s a pet-friendly motel,�� she said. ��If push comes to shove and they tell me to give up my pet, I��m not going to go.��
Jenny Niklaus of HomeFirst, one of the nonprofit groups that work with the city on the shelter program, said there may be an upside to the armory��s closure.
��Maybe there will be some cool stuff coming out of this that we should be doing more of that if we weren��t forced in this position we would never have considered,�� she said. ��I don��t think we should be scared of the change.��
