Carlos Pena left the Angels on Sunday, but he doesn't plan to leave the majors.
"I feel too good," Pena said after the Angels granted his release, per an out in his contract. "(Retirement) is not even an option right now. I have full intent to continue playing and look forward to having fun and playing ball and being successful."
Pena, 35, was brought to Angels camp on a minor league deal because he can play first base. As Albert Pujols proved himself to be capable of handling first base without much time off, there was less of a need for Pena. He didn't help his cause by hitting.139, with 14 strikeouts in 36 at-bats.
Pena said he felt like he was making progress at the plate, though.
"I'm just a click away from having a lot of fun," he said.
The Angels also released catcher Yorvit Torrealba and infielder Chad Tracy, who had the same contract out as Pena.
"It��s always a tough time in spring, with the conversations that go from guys having good spring to the conversations that we had today," Manager Mike Scioscia said. "It��s tough. Chad, Carlos and Yorvit – they��re pros and they can still play. I��m sure they��re going to find a fit somewhere."
The Angels also reassigned left-hander Wade LeBlanc to minor league camp, leaving them at 38 players in camp, with eight days to go before opening day.
In other Angels tidbits...
Jose Alvarez, the left-hander acquired from the Tigers for Andrew Romine, arrived in camp this morning. He said he is scheduled to throw three or four innings in a minor league game today. Alvarez also said that reports of him having surgery were false, although they were often repeated on the internet. He said he has never had surgery.
Hector Santiago was not at all concerned with allowing six runs in five innings in a minor league game on Saturday. As Santiago described it, the defense behind him was shaky, and it was also difficult to muster the normal intensity with a few dozen people watching on a back field. Santiago said he jumped in his pool at home in his full uniform after the game, to "washi it away." His next outing is scheduled to be on Thursday at Dodger Stadium.
