CLEVELAND — Three times David Lighty tried to leave the Quicken Loans Arena court after Ohio State's practice, and three times he was swept into the stands.
A teenage girl wanted a hug. A fan holding a No. 23 jersey wanted a high-five. Most of the arena, it seemed, wanted a picture taken with the neighborhood guy.
Lighty, a Cleveland native who attended Villa Angela-St. Joseph High School, couldn't have planned a better homecoming for himself and top-seeded Ohio State in the NCAA tournament.
"It's special," Lighty said. "Coming back to where we all started, playing in front of our family and friends and now pretty much end our career with them being able to see us is something you can't really describe."
The Big Ten champion Buckeyes (32-2) will try to avoid becoming the first No. 1 seed to lose to a No. 16 seed when they face Texas-San Antonio (20-13) on Friday. Eight of their 12 roster spots are filled by Ohio natives.
"We knew each other before we came to Ohio State, whether it be through AAU circuit or through playing each other during high school days," said senior center Dallas Lauderdale, from Solon in the Cleveland suburbs. "That's what made the commitment to Ohio State a lot easier and gave us the spirit to keep playing with each other. The relationships we've built have been more than just basketball relationships."
Coach Thad Matta's teams have become synonymous with outstanding freshmen, from Greg Oden and Mike Conley Jr. on the 2007 national runner-up team to this season's sensation, Jared Sullinger.
