A Vancouver businessman wanted on corruption and embezzlement charges in China has won the latest round of his legal fight to stay in Canada, with a Federal Court judge ordering his case be heard by a new panel of the Refugee Protection Division.
The ruling comes after lawyers for Mo Yeung (Michael) Ching appealed an October 2014 RPD decision which found Ching cannot avail himself of refugee protection in Canada because there are strong reasons for considering that Ching committed a serious non-political crime in China. This excludes him from the definition of a person in need of protection under Section 98 of the Immigration and Refugee Protection Act, the refugee panel determined.
Federal Court justice Yvan Roy overturned that decision in a ruling released Wednesday, criticizing the Refugee Protection Division for relying on untested evidence from a foreign court case in which Ching was not even a defendant.
That case involved a real estate transaction in Beijing which Chinese authorities say resulted in an embezzlement of 5.3 million Chinese yuan renminbi ($1.1 million CAD). The embezzlement allegation relates principally to two other Chinese nationals, but Ching is mentioned in the case. It is unclear whether the embezzlement occurred and whether Ching would have benefited, Roy wrote in his decision.
"Part of the difficulty in this case is the quality of the evidence on which the RPD relied to reach its conclusion," Roy wrote in the decision. "It is fuzzy and third-hand and, when considered carefully, does not implicate the applicant other than through bald statements made in foreign judgments."
